{"subjectId":"java","id":"iterator-cme","title":"Iterators & ConcurrentModificationException","group":"Collections Framework","order":47,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Removing from a collection while a for-each loop walks it throws <code>ConcurrentModificationException</code>. Use <code>removeIf</code>, <code>Iterator.remove</code>, or a concurrent collection.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>Iterator</strong> — an object with <code>hasNext()</code>, <code>next()</code> and optional <code>remove()</code> that walks a collection. The for-each loop over any <code>Iterable</code> is compiled into exactly these calls.</p></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-octagon\"></i>Fail-fast iterators</h3>\n<p><code>ArrayList</code>, <code>HashMap</code> and friends keep a counter <code>modCount</code>, bumped on every <em>structural</em> change (add/remove, not <code>set</code>). The iterator remembers the value when it was created and checks it on each <code>next()</code>; a mismatch throws.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        List&lt;Integer&gt; nums = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(List.of(1, 2, 3, 4));\n        try {\n            for (Integer n : nums) {\n                if (n % 2 == 0) nums.remove(n);   // structural change behind the iterator's back\n            }\n        } catch (ConcurrentModificationException e) {\n            System.out.println(\"ConcurrentModificationException\");\n        }\n        System.out.println(nums);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>ConcurrentModificationException\n[1, 3, 4]</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Despite the name, this has nothing to do with threads — one thread modifying a collection it is iterating is enough. It is also <em>best-effort</em>: removing the second-to-last element skips the final <code>hasNext()</code> check and silently exits without an exception, so never rely on it to catch bugs.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-check\"></i>Three correct ways to remove</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        List&lt;Integer&gt; a = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(List.of(1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6));\n        a.removeIf(n -&gt; n % 2 == 0);                  // 1. simplest (Java 8+)\n        System.out.println(a);\n\n        List&lt;Integer&gt; b = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(List.of(1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6));\n        for (Iterator&lt;Integer&gt; it = b.iterator(); it.hasNext(); ) {\n            if (it.next() % 2 == 0) it.remove();      // 2. the iterator's own remove\n        }\n        System.out.println(b);\n\n        List&lt;Integer&gt; c = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(List.of(1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6));\n        for (int i = c.size() - 1; i &gt;= 0; i--) {     // 3. index loop, backwards\n            if (c.get(i) % 2 == 0) c.remove(i);\n        }\n        System.out.println(c);\n\n        Map&lt;String, Integer&gt; m = new HashMap&lt;&gt;(Map.of(\"a\", 1, \"b\", 2));\n        m.entrySet().removeIf(e -&gt; e.getValue() &gt; 1); // maps: remove through a view\n        System.out.println(m);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>[1, 3, 5]\n[1, 3, 5]\n[1, 3, 5]\n{a=1}</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-left-right\"></i><code>ListIterator</code> — both directions, edit in place</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        List&lt;String&gt; words = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(List.of(\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"));\n        ListIterator&lt;String&gt; it = words.listIterator();\n        while (it.hasNext()) {\n            int i = it.nextIndex();\n            String w = it.next();\n            if (w.equals(\"b\")) {\n                it.set(\"B\");          // replace the element just returned\n                it.add(\"b2\");         // insert after it\n            }\n            System.out.print(i + \":\" + w + \" \");   // \"c\" is now at index 3\n        }\n        System.out.println();\n        while (it.hasPrevious()) System.out.print(it.previous() + \" \");\n        System.out.println();\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>0:a 1:b 3:c\nc b2 B a</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-shield\"></i>Fail-safe (snapshot / weakly consistent) iterators</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\nimport java.util.concurrent.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        List&lt;Integer&gt; cow = new CopyOnWriteArrayList&lt;&gt;(List.of(1, 2, 3));\n        for (Integer n : cow) {\n            if (n == 1) cow.add(99);      // iterator walks a snapshot — no exception\n            System.out.print(n + \" \");\n        }\n        System.out.println(\"-&gt; \" + cow);\n\n        Map&lt;Integer, String&gt; chm = new ConcurrentHashMap&lt;&gt;(Map.of(1, \"a\", 2, \"b\"));\n        for (Integer k : chm.keySet()) chm.remove(k);   // allowed\n        System.out.println(chm);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>1 2 3 -&gt; [1, 2, 3, 99]\n{}</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Fail-fast</th><th>Fail-safe / weakly consistent</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examples</td><td><code>ArrayList</code>, <code>HashMap</code>, <code>HashSet</code></td><td><code>CopyOnWriteArrayList</code>, <code>ConcurrentHashMap</code></td></tr><tr><td>Modify while iterating</td><td>Throws <code>ConcurrentModificationException</code></td><td>Allowed</td></tr><tr><td>Sees changes made during iteration?</td><td>—</td><td>COW: never (snapshot); CHM: maybe</td></tr><tr><td>Cost</td><td>None</td><td>COW copies the whole array on every write</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What causes ConcurrentModificationException and how do you avoid it?</summary><div><p>A fail-fast iterator notices <code>modCount</code> changed by something other than itself — typically <code>list.remove(x)</code> inside a for-each loop. Avoid it with <code>removeIf</code>, <code>Iterator.remove()</code>, collecting items to remove and calling <code>removeAll</code> afterwards, iterating over a copy, or using a concurrent collection.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Fail-fast vs fail-safe iterators?</summary><div><p>Fail-fast iterators (most <code>java.util</code> collections) throw on concurrent structural modification. Fail-safe ones (<code>java.util.concurrent</code>) never throw: <code>CopyOnWriteArrayList</code> iterates a snapshot, <code>ConcurrentHashMap</code> is weakly consistent — it reflects some, maybe all, changes made after the iterator was created.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Iterator vs ListIterator?</summary><div><p><code>Iterator</code> works on any collection, forward only, with <code>remove</code>. <code>ListIterator</code> is for lists: moves both ways (<code>previous</code>), knows indices (<code>nextIndex</code>), and can <code>set</code> and <code>add</code> at the cursor.</p></div></details></section>"}